praise:
The keyframe itself does what the brief asks, and I verified it rather than taking the report's word for it. Outside the replaced parchment field, f1983 equals the live mf030 capture bit for bit in both buckets (my own diff: max 0, 0 px). On the rebuilt field I found:
- no rectangular tone step in a x6 low-frequency stretch;
- no seam in a x10 high-pass;
- edge-band high-pass energy identical to the live card (3.83 vs 3.83 on 16:9, 4.97 vs 4.98 on 4:3);
- field mean RGB within about 1-2/255 of the live parchment.

At 100 % the blank card reads as real vellum, with soft mottling, foxing specks and the inner dark line intact. It does not look painted over, so there is no sticker or plastic feel. The brass corners and walnut frame are left untouched. The chrome mattes are clean straight-alpha cut-outs with no halo or fringe on either black or white, and the tassels, fringe and lion silhouettes come through intact. Choosing the settled frame over the literal frame 1 was the right call for the still. Porting the layout to Godot's own rounding and nine-patch maths, instead of guessing, is AAA-grade craft. The doc's 627x661 card was wrong, and catching it saves the leaf landing downstream. The reporting is honest: deviations, SwiftShader vs iPad, and heuristic flame masks are all disclosed. What keeps this from a 9+ is not the pixels. It is that the hand-off motion, which is what the client is really approving, was assumed rather than checked. The overlay as written starts the dissolve during the nameplate pop-in.
